  if (!display || display === 'none') {{\n            console.log([gd, 'removed!']);\n            Plotly.purge(gd);\n            observer.disconnect();\n        }}\n}});\n\n// Listen for the removal of the full notebook cells\nvar notebookContainer = gd.closest('#notebook-container');\nif (notebookContainer) {{\n    x.observe(notebookContainer, {childList: true});\n}}\n\n// Listen for the clearing of the current output cell\nvar outputEl = gd.closest('.output');\nif (outputEl) {{\n    x.observe(outputEl, {childList: true});\n}}\n\n                        })                };            </script>        </div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Odds ratios\n\nIf $\\beta_1=0.7$, a one-unit increase in $x$ multiplies the odds by\n\n$$\ne^{0.7}\\approx2.01.\n$$\n\nThat is an odds statement, not a doubling of probability.\n\n## DS/ML Bridge\n\nLogistic regression teaches several ideas that survive in modern classifiers:\n\n```text\nprobability output\nloss / likelihood\ndecision threshold\nprecision-recall tradeoff\nROC behavior\ncalibration\nclass imbalance\n```\n\nA high AUC does not guarantee well-calibrated probabilities. Ranking quality and probability quality are distinct.\n\n## Summary\n- Logistic regression maps a linear score into $(0,1)$.\n- Coefficients act additively on log-odds and multiplicatively on odds.\n- Classification thresholds are decision choices, not intrinsic model truths.\n- Evaluate both discrimination and calibration when probabilities matter.\n\n**Next:** *Bayesian Statistics — Learning by Updating*\n\n",
